introduction:
  bigQuestion: "Who defends the network when you sleep?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on <strong>Architectural Abstraction</strong> and <strong>Proactive Defense</strong>. While SL covered basic connections, HL dives into the 'How'—specifically the orchestration of the <strong>TCP/IP Stack</strong>, the logic of <strong>Dynamic Routing</strong>, and active countermeasures like <strong>IDS</strong> and <strong>IPS</strong>."
  journey: "You'll start by dissecting the 4-layer <strong>TCP/IP Model</strong> (Encapsulation). Then you'll explore enterprise architecture (Reverse Proxies), and finally, you'll learn how to secure it all with <strong>Intrusion Prevention Systems</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "TCP/IP Stack (The Model)"
    icon: "fa-layer-group"
    content: |
      <p>The rules of the internet. Data is wrapped in envelopes (Headers) as it moves down.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Application:</strong> Data.</li>
          <li><strong>Transport:</strong> Segments (Reliability).</li>
          <li><strong>Network:</strong> Packets (Addressing).</li>
          <li><strong>Link/Physical:</strong> Frames (Hardware).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Server Architecture"
    icon: "fa-server"
    content: |
      <p>How do big sites handle millions of users?</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Proxy Server:</strong> Hides the Client (Anonymity).</li>
          <li><strong>Reverse Proxy:</strong> Hides the Server (Security & Load Balancing).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Dynamic Routing"
    icon: "fa-route"
    content: |
      <p>The internet is self-healing.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Routing Table:</strong> A map of the network stored on the router.</li>
          <li><strong>Dynamic:</strong> If a cable is cut, the router automatically recalculates a new "Optimal Path" in seconds.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Active Defense (IDS/IPS)"
    icon: "fa-shield-virus"
    content: |
      <p>Firewalls are just gates. We need guards.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>IDS (Detection):</strong> The Alarm. Watches traffic and alerts admin.</li>
          <li><strong>IPS (Prevention):</strong> The Bouncer. Watches traffic and actively BLOCKS threats.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "TCP/IP Model"
    definition: "The conceptual model and set of communications protocols used in the Internet (4 Layers)."
  - term: "Encapsulation"
    definition: "The process of adding headers/footers to data as it moves down the protocol stack."
  - term: "PDU (Protocol Data Unit)"
    definition: "The specific unit of data at a layer (Segment, Packet, Frame)."
  - term: "Reverse Proxy"
    definition: "A server that sits in front of web servers and forwards client requests to those web servers (Load balancing/Security)."
  - term: "Dynamic Routing"
    definition: "A process where routers automatically exchange information to update their routing tables to find the best path."
  - term: "IDS (Intrusion Detection System)"
    definition: "A device or software application that monitors a network or system for malicious activity or policy violations (Alerts only)."
  - term: "IPS (Intrusion Prevention System)"
    definition: "A network security tool that continuously monitors network traffic for malicious activity and takes action to prevent it (Blocks)."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "IDS Blocks Hackers"
    correction: "No. An IDS is just a burglar alarm. It screams, but it doesn't lock the door. You need an IPS (Prevention System) to block."
  - mistake: "Proxy = VPN"
    correction: "A Proxy hides your IP for one specific app (like a Browser). A VPN encrypts your ENTIRE connection."
  - mistake: "Shortest Path = Best Path"
    correction: "Not always. The shortest cable might be congested. Dynamic routers look for the 'Lowest Cost' path (Speed + Reliability), not just distance."

